.UNINDENT
.sp
These options alter the XML for a single class of XML elements. More complete documentation is found in virt\-install(1).
.sp
Generally these options map pretty straightforwardly to the libvirt XML, documented at \%<https://\:libvirt\:.org/\:formatdomain\:.html>
.sp
Option strings are in the format of: \-\-option opt=val,opt2=val2,...  example: \-\-disk path=/tmp/foo,shareable=on. Properties can be used with \(aq\-\-option opt=,\(aq, so to clear a disks cache setting you could use \(aq\-\-disk cache=,\(aq
.sp
For any option, use \-\-option=? to see a list of all available sub options, example: \-\-disk=?  or  \-\-boot=?
.sp
\-\-help output also lists a few general examples. See the EXAMPLES section below for some common examples.
.sp
virt\-xml specifically has some operations that don\(aqt really apply to virt\-install
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB\-\-boot refresh\-machine\-type=yes\fP
Refresh the XML \fB<os><type machine=X></os>\fP value to the latest one
that qemu provides. For example, if your VM has a machine type value
\fBpc\-q35\-4.0\fP, this will reset the value to \fBq35\fP, and works
similarly with other versioned machine types. Occasionally this is
necessary to get enable qemu bug fixes, or when qemu deprecates and
removes old machine type values.
.UNINDENT
.SH MISCELLANEOUS OPTIONS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB\-h\fP, \fB\-\-help\fP
Show the help message and exit
.TP
.B \fB\-\-version\fP
Show program\(aqs version number and exit
.TP
.B \fB\-q\fP, \fB\-\-quiet\fP
Avoid verbose output.
.TP
.B \fB\-d\fP, \fB\-\-debug\fP
Print debugging information
.UNINDENT
.SH "مثال‌ها (EXAMPLES)"
.sp
See a list of all suboptions that \-\-disk and \-\-network take
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml \-\-disk=? \-\-network=?
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Change the <description> of domain \(aqEXAMPLE\(aq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml EXAMPLE \-\-edit \-\-metadata description=\(dqmy new description\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
# Enable the boot device menu for domain \(aqEXAMPLE\(aq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml EXAMPLE \-\-edit \-\-boot menu=on
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Clear the previous <cpu> definition of domain \(aqwinxp\(aq, change it to \(aqhost\-model\(aq, but interactively confirm the diff before saving:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml winxp \-\-edit \-\-cpu host\-model,clearxml=yes \-\-confirm
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Change the second sound card to model=ich6 on \(aqfedora19\(aq, but only output the diff:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml fedora19 \-\-edit 2 \-\-sound model=ich6 \-\-print\-diff
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Update the every graphics device password to \(aqfoo\(aq of the running VM \(aqrhel6\(aq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml rhel6 \-\-edit all \-\-graphics password=foo \-\-update
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Remove the disk path from disk device hdc:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml rhel6 \-\-edit target=hdc \-\-disk path=
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Change all disk devices of type \(aqdisk\(aq to use cache=none, using XML from stdin, printing the new XML to stdout.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# cat <xmlfile> | virt\-xml \-\-edit device=disk \-\-disk cache=none
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Change disk \(aqhda\(aq IO to native and use startup policy as \(aqoptional\(aq.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml fedora20 \-\-edit target=hda \e
           \-\-disk io=native,startup_policy=optional
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Change all host devices to use driver_name=vfio for VM \(aqfedora20\(aq on the remote connection
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml \-\-connect qemu+ssh://remotehost/system \e
           fedora20 \-\-edit all \-\-hostdev driver_name=vfio
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Hotplug host USB device 001.003 to running domain \(aqfedora19\(aq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml fedora19 \-\-update \-\-add\-device \-\-hostdev 001.003
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Add a spicevmc channel to the domain \(aqwinxp\(aq, that will be available after the next VM shutdown.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml winxp \-\-add\-device \-\-channel spicevmc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Create a 10G qcow2 disk image and attach it to \(aqfedora18\(aq for the next VM startup:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml fedora18 \-\-add\-device \e
  \-\-disk /var/lib/libvirt/images/newimage.qcow2,format=qcow2,size=10
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Same as above, but ensure the disk is attached to the most appropriate bus
for the guest OS by providing information about it on the command line:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml fedora18 \-\-osinfo fedora18 \-\-add\-device \e
  \-\-disk /var/lib/libvirt/images/newimage.qcow2,format=qcow2,size=10
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Hotunplug the disk vdb from the running domain \(aqrhel7\(aq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml rhel7 \-\-update \-\-remove\-device \-\-disk target=vdb
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Remove all graphics devices from the VM \(aqrhel7\(aq after the next shutdown:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml rhel7 \-\-remove\-device \-\-graphics all
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Generate XML for a virtio console device and print it to stdout:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml \-\-build\-xml \-\-console pty,target_type=virtio
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Add qemu command line passthrough:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml f25 \-\-edit \-\-confirm \-\-qemu\-commandline=\(dq\-device FOO\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Use boot device \(aqnetwork\(aq for a single transient boot:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# virt\-xml myvm \-\-no\-define \-\-start \-\-edit \-\-boot network
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH "هشدارها (CAVEATS)"
.sp
Virtualization hosts supported by libvirt may not permit all changes that might seem possible. Some edits made to a VM\(aqs definition may be ignored. For instance, QEMU does not allow the removal of certain devices once they\(aqve been defined.
